 { Piece activity can be viewed through either a positional or a dynamic lens. No matter which perspective you take, one thing is for sure - if you don't pay close attention to this side of the game you are in for a tough time at the chess board! As a general rule, you should seek to combine two main goals. The first is to maximise your piece activity. The second is to reduce the activity of your opponent's pieces. In this position, let's see what happened when White made a careless exchange, allowing Black's pieces to quickly burst into the game. } 20. { -KEY- } Nxe6 $2 { Exchanging off Black's passive bishop is a bad idea, especially because now the Black rooks will enter the fray. } ( 20. Qd1 $14 { White needed to play a patient move such as this one. The queen will transfer to c1 next, safeguarding the vulnerable b2 pawn. Black is solid here, but White can hope for a slight advantage due to the pin along the e-file which keeps Black's heavy pieces somewhat passive. } ) 
Rxe6 $1 { White's poor decision releases the energy of all the heavy pieces, with the Black rooks active on the e-file while Black's queen now has an open field of vision along the g1-a7 diagonal. } ( 20... fxe6 $2 { This move would have returned the favour. It's much more important for Black to release the activity of their rooks than to worry about protecting their d5 pawn. } ) 
21. Nd4 ( 21. Rxe6 { wouldn't save White, since after } Rxe6 22. Rxe6 ( 22. Rb1 { does not save the pawn, since after } Qf2 $19 { Black's active pieces create more threats than White can deal with. } ) 
Qxe6 $19 { White will lose the b2 pawn since they have to take time to stop the main threat of ...Qe1+. } ) 
Nxb2 $1 { The piece pressure leads to the loss of the pawn. } 22. Qc2 Rxe2 23. Rxe2 Rxe2 24. Qxe2 Nc4 $19 { Black returns, having stolen a clean pawn and leaving White to worry about the nasty threat of ...Qb1+. When your opponent's pieces converge around a particular square, in this case e6, it pays to be careful to not release that energy without having a very compelling reason. Doing so can cause your position to collapse in a flash, as happened to White after their careless first move. In this section, we're going to be focusing on three main themes: 1. Passivity, and how to benefit from it. 2. Paralysis, a type of extreme piece passivity. 3. Piece manoeuvres Let's dive in! } 
 *

