 { Piece passivity is rarely a good thing, but it's important to distinguish between short-term, medium-term and permanent passivity. The last of these is often fatal, while temporary passivity is rarely so. Let's look at a case of short-term passivity, taken from the game Oswaldo Cruz, W - Apsenieks,F (1939). } 30. { -KEY- } Nxh1 $1 { Avoiding the exchange of rooks is a smart decision, keeping the game alive. } ( 30. Rxh1 $6 { This move would throw away all the practical chances. } Rxh1 31. Nxh1 axb3 32. axb3 f6 $11 { Too much material has come off the board, and so the endgame is equal. } ) 
axb3 31. axb3 Ra8 { At first glance it might seem as though Black stands better here. They have an open file with an entry square on a2, while White's knight sits passively in the corner. However, appearances can be deceiving! } 32. Nf2 { White begins the journey of turning his passive knight into the best piece on the board! } Ra2 33. Ne4 $1 Bd5 { The only move to stay alive, shutting down the open file. } ( 33... Rxc2 $4 { The pawn cannot be taken: } 34. Rd8 $18 { Black is helpless against White's idea to build a mating net against the enemy king. } ) 
34. Nf6 $1 { The knight quickly morphs from the most passive piece on the board to the most active! Spotting the outpost on f6 at the starting position was key to give White the confidence to keep the rooks on the board, as now mating ideas become possible if Black gets careless. } Rxc2 35. Ra1 $1 { White is doing everything right, posing serious practical problems. Only one move saves Black, and it's not an easy one to find... } Rc3+ $4 { In a difficult position, Black goes fatally wrong. } ( 35... g5 $1 { The only path, and not at all easy to find. Here's the idea: } 36. fxg5 Rc3 37. Kh4 Bg2 $1 { Black distracts White from the mating net by setting up one of his own! } 38. Nh5 Kf8 39. Nf4 Bd5 $11 { Since White has lost the chance to set up a mating net, the equal pawn endgame is drawn with best play. } ) 
36. Kh4 { Now ...g5 ideas vanish as the White king joins the attack. } Bg2 37. Ra8 Rh3+ 38. Kg5 Rh8 { The only way to stop Rg8#. } ( { However, now there are several ways to win. In the game White played } 38... Rh8 38. Ne8 { but perhaps even cleaner is the following line: } ) 
39. Rxh8 Kxh8 40. e6 $1 fxe6 41. Kxg6 Bf3 42. Kf7 $18 { Black cannot stop the advance of the g-pawn. In this example we saw that temporary passivity is not always something to be afraid of. The knight was inferior for a couple of moves, but had the greatest long-term potential once it could be activated. The key question White had to consider was whether or not Black would be able to make use of that brief window of opportunity to drum up some counterplay. Let's take a look at a more serious, medium-term example of piece passivity. } 
