 { White to play. Can you find the only path to an advantage? } 9. { -KEY- } Nb5 $1 { Correct! The cheap threat of giving a check on c7 can be stopped, but it will come at a cost. } Na6 { There was no other way to cover the c7 square. The problem for Black is that now it's going to be a long time before the knight can be manoeuvred back into the game. } 10. c3 $1 { Setting up the perfect pawn chain, blocking any forward jumps of the knight. } Be7 11. dxc5 $1 { This move is a lot stranger to understand. Why would White give up their ideal central structure? } Bxc5 12. b4 $1 { This is the point! White keeps the same coverage over the b4 and c5 squares, but now with options to expand further on the queenside. } Be7 13. h3 $1 { A good moment to challenge Black's bishop, as they cannot maintain the pin. } Bxf3 ( 13... Bh5 $2 14. g4 $1 Bg6 15. Bxg6 $18 { White stands much much better, having damaged Black's structure. This particular idea will be explored later in the course, in the chapter on 'Pawn Play'. } ) 
14. Qxf3 $16 { White is not far from an objectively winning position, having collected queenside space, the bishop pair, and condemned Black's a6 knight to passivity over the coming moves. One possible plan in the future for White will be to play a2-a3 and then c3-c4, to break in the centre and crack open the position for their bishop pair. The type of medium term passivity we saw here isn't always fatal, but it can be dangerous enough that it pays off to stay alert to any opportunity to inflict this kind of damage on the opponent. } 
