 { Paralysis happens much more frequently in the endgame, so we'll revisit the topic then. However, it can also happen in the middlegame in surprising ways. At first glance, the position seems fine for Black. They have a central and well-supported knight, and both of their bishops appear to sit on good squares. However, White's next move comes as a cold shower. } 1. { -KEY- } c5 $1 { White grabs space on the queenside. The real problem though is that now the Black knight is stuck on e4. This sets off a nasty chain effect on several other minor pieces. No matter what Black tries, they're stuck. Let's analyse a few options. } Nd7 { Since Black has no way of breaking the bind, they decide to give up the defense of the e4 knight. } ( 1... Bg6 $2 { This natural move can be easily refuted by White. } 2. h3 $1 { White can ignore the bishop on g6 for now. } Nh5 { Although Black's bishop from g6 for now defends the piece on e4, the problem is that it can be immediately removed. } 3. Nxg6 hxg6 4. Bh2 $1 { Preventing Black from playing the desired ...Nxf4 on the next move. } Nhf6 { Black has nothing better than to retreat the knight in order to support e4. However, this leaves Black in the same state of affairs as in our starting position, although now with White having secured the bishop pair along the way. } ( 4... Nef6 $2 { The knight still cannot retreat from the poisoned e4 square. } 5. g4 $18 { Black's knight on h5 is now lost. } ) 
5. b4 $16 { White has gained the bishop pair and grabbed a bunch of space, while Black's knights remain glued to e4 and f6. } ) 
 ( 1... Bxf3 $2 { The cure is worse than the disease. Black might break the paralysis, but they will ruin their king's defenses: } 2. gxf3 $1 g5 { The only way to avoid losing the knight on e4. } 3. Bg3 Nxg3 4. hxg3 $18 { If White plays well, he'll be able to set up a winning attack in the coming moves. } ) 
2. Nxd7 Qxd7 3. Ne5 { White improves the knight before thinking about taking on e4. } Qd8 4. Bxe4 dxe4 5. Nc4 $1 { A clever shift of focus. White avoids rushing to grab the pawn, instead turning their attention to the central outpost. } ( 5. Qxe4 $2 { Capturing the pawn would fall into Black's sneaky trap: } g5 $1 6. Bg3 f5 $1 7. Qd3 f4 $13 { White can save the piece with some complicated tactical play along the g-file, but the advantage has disappeared. } ) 
Qd5 6. Bd6 $1 { Eliminating Black's bishop pair will make it impossible for Black to dislodge the knight from d6. } Rfe8 7. a4 { Grabbing space. } Bg6 { Defending the e4 pawn. } ( 7... Bxd6 8. Nxd6 $16 { will cost Black material. } ) 
8. a5 { The push of the pawn has left Black with a backward pawn on b7. } Bxd6 9. Nxd6 Re7 10. Ra4 $16 { White has everything in their favor, from the better minor piece to more space to the healthier structure. The important thing to note is that all of Black's current problems stemmed from having been caught in an unusual case of middlegame paralysis. Let's take a look at a more typical scenario next, the kind that you're much more likely to be able to spot in your own games. } 
