 { Taken from an online game between Magnus Carlsen with the White pieces and GM Daniel Naroditsky with Black. Can you find the only winning move in the position for White? } 1. { -KEY- } Qf6 $1 ( { A crushing move, immediately winning. Black's only good defensive setup involved playing } 1. Qf6 Qf7 { , since in this case his king would be less exposed while his bishop on e8 would be hitting White's b5 knight. Now this configuration becomes impossible. } ) 
Bg6 ( { If } 1... Qf7 $2 { then } 2. Qxf7 Kxf7 3. Nxc7 $18 { with a winning endgame for White. } ) 
2. Nd4 $1 { Threatening to bring the knight to e6, from where the c7 pawn will be attacked and mate would be threatened on f8. } Qf7 { There is nothing better, but this allows a cute tactical shot. } 3. Qd8+ $1 Kg7 4. Qxc7 $1 { Winning the pawn by force! } Kf6 ( { If } 4... Qxc7 { then } 5. Ne6 $18 { leads to the same winning endgame that we mentioned earlier. } ) 
5. Qxd6+ { Black's position is hopeless, and a few moves later Black resigned. This game showcased an important point, namely that your piece activity and the opponent's passivity are often interconnected. By bringing the queen to f6, White increased their piece activity and at the same time thwarted Black's most active configuration. There are two more topics that I wish to quickly address here, although they will not be explored in depth as they are covered elsewhere in the course. } 
