 { The first topic to quickly touch upon is that of sacrificing for piece activity. We'll be studying this in depth later on in the chapter, so let's just show one example for now. This position is taken from the game Kasparov,G - Leko,P (2003) . We can see that both of White's bishops are locked in due to the pawn structure. Can you find the move that Kasparov played in order to activate his bishops? } 25. { -KEY- } Rxe5 $1 { By sacrificing the exchange, White breaks down the central structure. } dxe5 26. Rxe5 Rxc4 $1 { Leko returns the favour! This is an important move, since otherwise White was ready to capture on d4 when both of his bishops would have entered the game to great effect. } 27. dxc4 Qxc4 { Material is once again equal, as is each side's piece activity. } 28. Re2 Bxc2 29. d6 $1 { Another important sacrifice, this time to activate the queen. } exd6 30. Rd2 d3 31. Qxb7 Rf5 32. Qd7 { Threatening mate on d8. } f6 33. Qxd6+ Kf7 $11 { White has nothing better than to keep checking the king, so the players agreed to a draw. Sensing the positional danger and being willing to sacrifice material is a really valuable skill, and we'll be touching upon the various kinds of sacrifices you can expect to come across in sections four and five of this chapter. } 
