 { The final technique to revisit is that of maneuvering for piece activity. We'll only show a couple of examples of this since we have already run into similar situations in the 'Weak squares' and 'Outposts' chapters earlier on. In this first example, can you find the strong 2-move sequence that White played? } 1. { -KEY- } Nd2 $1 { The knight is headed to c4! Finding the right maneuver is not only about activating your pieces. It's also about spotting other positional features of the position. } Re8 2. Nc4 $1 { The knight is ideally placed on the weak square at c4. Not only is it much more active than on f3, it now also points at the main weakness of the Black position - the d6 pawn. } Bf8 { Black is forced on the defensive. } 3. Nb5 $1 { Adding to the pressure. } Rd7 { Scrambling to defend the pawn, but losing coordination in the process. White capitalises on this with their next move. } 4. Ne3 $1 { The bishop's retreat has been blocked off by the rook. Black is in trouble since if they allow 5.Nxf5 their structure will be in ruins. } Be4 { Black must avoid losing the bishop, as then their structure would be ruined. } 5. Nc3 $1 Nc7 6. Qd2 $1 { Preparing Rae1 followed by f2-f3, targeting the stranded bishop. } ( 6. f3 $2 { Trying to win the bishop immediately fails tactically because of the x-ray along the e-file. } Bxd5 $1 7. Ncxd5 Ncxd5 8. Nxd5 Nxd5 9. Qxd5 Rxe2 $17 { Black has recovered their pawn and even stands better now, due to their active rook and safer king. } ) 
Nfxd5 { Black has nothing better than to accept the loss of an exchange: } 7. Ncxd5 Nxd5 8. Nxd5 Bxd5 9. Bb5 $18 { Again, Black's poor piece coordination (resulting from defending the weak d6 pawn) comes back to haunt them. White emerges a clean exchange to the good. The reason why the manouevre was so effective was that it both ramped up White's piece activity and took advantage of the other key features of the position, like the weak square at c4 and Black's vulnerable pawn on d6. Let's look at one more similar example. } 
